# A tuple of the wrong shape is a type mismatch against the written tuple type.
Cannot convert argument of type 'tuple[int32, int32, int32]' to 'tuple[int32, str]' @5:27
# Bare tuple(t) still names the arity limitation and advises dropping the conversion (#1159).
'tuple(...)' cannot convert an iterable to a tuple @8:16
# The conversion takes exactly one argument — a tuple is not built from separate elements here.
'tuple[int32, str](...)' expects exactly 1 argument (the tuple to convert) but got 2 @10:13
# Uncalled, it is a type reference like any other and cannot be a value (#1192's rule, #1200's kind).
a generic type reference must be constructed; 'tuple[...]' cannot be used as a value @12:13
